WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.980
The third season of Stranger Things was a
ton of fun, but that doesn’t mean it was

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.800
flawless, so we’re breaking down season
three to find the high points and the low

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.300
points.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.080
From "interminable bickering" to fresh comic
relief, here are the three best and three

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.440
worst things about Stranger Things season
three.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.560
Making things bigger is a classic element
of serialized cinema.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.200
Simply put, you can't go on entertaining the
masses with the same stuff that hooked them

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.160
in the first place.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.630
Showrunners, directors, and producers are
always working on ways to make things newer,

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.660
better, and badder.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:33.540
On top of that, the third season is an interesting
point in any series, as it serves as a transition

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.360
point of sorts.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.980
On the one hand, it typically connects to
original plots from the beginning of the show,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.120
while on the other, it attempts to reorient
and expand the story to keep things entertaining

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.180
and map out future possibilities.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.220
This is always a difficult task, but in the
case of Stranger Things season three, the

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.550
production crew has actually managed to calibrate
and graduate the story to greater heights

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.100
without losing the cozy, small-town feel of
the first season.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.300
Season three clearly has some pretty big fish
to fry, like the invasion of the Mind Flayer

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.010
and the unveiling of a huge Russian base underneath
Hawkins.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.130
However, it masterfully balances these major
plot points with a healthy dose of carnival

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:11.881
fun, trips to the mall, a deep dive into the
relationships of awkward teenagers, and even

00:01:11.881 --> 00:01:15.180
a hilarious reference to the massively overblown
New Coke fiasco.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.220
"How do you even drink that?"

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.640
"Because it's delicious."

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.180
"What!?"

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.060
It's incredibly sweet to watch as Eleven and
Mike date, break up, and then find their way

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.060
back to each other.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.740
Lucas and Max get in on the fun, too, with
their sage relationship advice.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.270
Plus, Dustin keeps us endlessly entertained
as he loyally maintains his season-long quest

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.340
to link up with Suzie.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.840
"Think Phoebe Cates, only hotter."

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.420
But there's one relationship throughout the
series that's just beating a dead horse.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.549
We're talking, of course, about the unspoken
attraction, disgust, tension - whatever you

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:47.660
want to call it - between Chief Jim Hopper
and Joyce Byers.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.400
The two have been flirting since the earliest
days of the show, and by the end of season

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.680
three, they still haven't managed to hook
up or call it off.

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:58.439
Season two had Bob Newby, Joyce's boyfriend,
who eased the tension and served as a natural

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.080
barrier between the two characters.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.950
There was no such firewall in season three,
and the banter the two engaged in as they

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.820
traveled together got old really fast.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.080
"That's why you should have listened to Alexei."

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.380
"Oh yeah, right, your new boyfriend, right?"

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.980
"Yes, every man I talk to from now on has
to be my boyfriend."

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.420
Murray Bauman said it best in episode seven,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.260
"Children!

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:17.920
Children!

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.240
Children!

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.480
This interminable bickering was amusing at
first, but it's getting very stale."

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.300
Lucas Sinclair's little sister was in season
two, but season three is where her star really

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:30.800
shines.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.120
Erica Sinclair is all sass and sharp comebacks
...

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.560
"Isn't it past your bedtime?"

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.680
"Isn't it time you die?"

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.740
Erica is also keen negotiator.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.090
As the kids plan to infiltrate the secret
Russian operation within the mall, they attempt

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.450
to recruit the little hellraiser due to her
smaller physique, as she can crawl through

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.160
the air ducts.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.959
Sensing an opportunity, Erica has them bring
her all of the ice cream she could possibly

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.480
desire as they discuss her potential involvement.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.420
Erica ends up being a steady presence throughout
the season, nearly always delivering a bit

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.980
of comic relief that cuts through the tension
like butter.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.460
Her bump in screen time isn't an accident,
either.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.940
In an interview with Vulture after season
two had just dropped, writer and director

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:08.530
Matt Duffer said she totally earned it, revealing,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.280
"Any excuse we had to get Erica in there,
we'd write her in."

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.041
The character's role in season three is so
entertaining and scene-stealing that it even

00:03:15.041 --> 00:03:18.580
pushed Entertainment Weekly to dub her the
MVP of the season.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.000
One of the hallmark features of the Stranger
Things storyline is the occasional trips to

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:23.860
the Upside Down.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:28.480
The first two seasons feature multiple forays
into that gloomy, dangerous, inverted world.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.610
The third season goes there, too, as Billy
Hargrove meets his other-worldly counterpart

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.100
just after his mind is flayed.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.000
But after that?

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.640
The Upside Down nearly leaves the story entirely.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.370
To be fair, it makes sense.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.220
After all, the Mind Flayer is doing its level
best throughout the season to invade the normal,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.160
"right-side up" world.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.500
It follows that the majority of the season
would take place in the real world as the

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.800
flayed carry out their master's bidding and
form a bloody, mutilated mega-corpse for it

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:52.300
to use.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.220
Nevertheless, the lack of time spent in the
Upside Down in season three ends up being

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.460
a bit of a disappointment.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.640
Throughout the season, the possibility of
running into Demogorgons or Demodogs in their

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.870
own element remains firmly out of reach.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:08.160
Instead, the utter and complete mystery of
the Upside Down remains just that - a mystery.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.120
Conspiracy theorist Murray Bauman appeared
earlier in the series when he was hired as

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:17.009
a private investigator by the Hollands to
find their missing daughter, fan favorite

00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:17.500
Barbara.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.880
From there, he ended up helping Nancy Wheeler
and Jonathan Byers as they attempted to expose

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.500
the Hawkins National Laboratory.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:24.340
"What are you doing?"

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.300
"Thinking."

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.060
"With vodka?"

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.480
"It's a central nervous system depressant,
so yes."

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.020
Bauman's role was fun, but auxiliary at best,
until season three.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.220
Much like Erica Sinclair, season three sees
Bauman became a much bigger player.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.059
His character is integral to the entire second
half of the season, as he gives asylum to

00:04:41.059 --> 00:04:45.619
Hopper and Joyce, helps infiltrate the Russian
base under the Starcourt Mall, and translates

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.280
for Alexei, who quickly becomes Murray's friend.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.159
Murray's sweet but short-lived relationship
with Alexei was the surprise friendship of

00:04:52.159 --> 00:04:55.550
the season, on par with Dustin and Steve's
unlikely bond.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:59.490
Too bad poor Alexei didn't survive the carnival
- Murray, who's typically a loner, really

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.160
seemed to take a liking to the guy.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:03.960
"No."

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.580
Hopper is awesome.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.120
The dude is nearly as good at stopping bad
guys as he is bad at being a parent.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:18.590
But the truth is, he's a small-town law enforcement
officer, not Rambo, and the endless stream

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.300
of villains he takes on throughout season
three gets way out of hand when you add it

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:22.800
all up.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.280
From an ongoing string of fistfights and shootouts
with the Russians to his multi-episode duel

00:05:27.289 --> 00:05:31.689
with the Soviet equivalent of the Terminator,
Hopper comes across as an unstoppable juggernaut

00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:32.780
in season three.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.820
No matter how badly each fight goes, he always
comes back for more.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.860
No matter how beat up he is, by the next episode,
he's swinging away like he's still in round

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:40.360
one.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.020
Even the fact that he may have survived the
explosion in the Russian base - an event that

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:48.559
vaporized Soviet agents standing much further
away from the blast - boggles the imagination.

00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:52.850
Plus, why did he wait until the events of
episode six to call for backup, considering

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.800
he was getting his butt kicked and running
from Russian soldiers multiple episodes prior?

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:58.520
"Because you're a policeman.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.779
Policemen have rules."

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:02.800
"Oh yeah?

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.280
You wanna test that theory?"

